<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuhwa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lady Yuhwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuhwa is the mother of Jumong, the founder of Goguryeo, in Korean legend. Described as the daughter of the river god Habaek, she was taken in by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geumwa">Geumwa</a> of Eastern Buyeo and gave birth to Jumong. She was later worshipped in Goguryeo as an ancestral goddess.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuhwa.jpg" alt="Yuhwa" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_Yuhwa_and_young_Chumo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>She appears under the names Habaengnyeo or Habaengnyeorang, the daughter of Habaek, and is given the name Yuhwa in the Samguk Sagi and the Samguk Yusa. In the legend Habaek, god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Amnok River</a>, had three daughters, Yuhwa, Hwonhwa and Wihwa. When Hae Mo-su came upon the sisters playing by the river, they fled, so he built a richly decorated palace and held a banquet to lure them. Once they had drunk, he blocked the way out and captured Yuhwa.</p><p>Habaek sent a messenger to rebuke him, and Hae Mo-su, ashamed, offered to let her go, but Yuhwa had fallen in love with him and refused to leave. He drove to Habaek's palace in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> drawn by five dragons, where Habaek challenged him to a contest of transformation: as a carp, a pheasant and a deer the god was caught each time by Hae Mo-su as an otter, a hawk and a wolf. Habaek then held a wedding feast.</p><p>When the couple were drunk, Habaek put them in a leather bag and sent them up towards Heaven in the dragon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, but Hae Mo-su woke on the way, cut the bag open with Yuhwa's golden hairpin and went on to Heaven alone. Habaek saw his returning daughter as a disgrace, had her lips stretched and exiled her with two servants to Wubalsu, a pond south of Taebaek Mountain.</p><p>A fisherman told King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geumwa">Geumwa</a> that a strange creature was moving under the water, and Yuhwa was pulled out on his orders; her lips had to be cut three times before she could speak. Recognising her as the concubine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a>, the king kept her in a detached palace, where the sunlight followed her and made her pregnant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After her death Lady Yuhwa was worshipped as one of Goguryeo's national gods together with her son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dongmyeong-of-goguryeo">King Dongmyeong</a>, the founder of the kingdom, and was identified with Susin, the goddess of caves, a name meaning cave deity. In recent times she was portrayed by Oh Yeon-soo in the MBC television series Jumong, broadcast in 2006 and 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lady Yuhwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the legend Geumwa took in <b>Yuhwa</b>, mother of Jumong, who later fled south to found Goguryeo.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-buyeo">Eastern Buyeo</a></li></ul></section>
